<section
  id="case-portfolio-logotype"
  class="fullscreen-section case-study-section"
  data-title="Case Study: KT Portfolio — Logo Design | Katja Turnsek"
  data-description="Graphic design case study for a hand-drawn KT mark refined into a fluid, scalable identity for digital and print applications."
  data-case="portfolio"
  aria-labelledby="case-portfolio-logotype-title"
>
  <div class="rows">
    <div class="row">
      <div class="case-study-header">
        <p class="case-eyebrow">Graphic Design</p>
        <h1 id="case-portfolio-logotype-title">KT Portfolio</h1>
        <p class="case-subtitle">A hand-drawn initials mark shaped into a
          distinctive personal identity for design and development work.</p>

        <dl class="case-summary" aria-label="Project summary">
          <div>
            <dt>Role</dt>
            <dd>Identity and logo design</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Scope</dt>
            <dd>Independent portfolio project</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Year</dt>
            <dd>2025–2026</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Tools</dt>
            <dd>Pencil and paper, Adobe Illustrator</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Outcome</dt>
            <dd>Responsive personal logo suite</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>

        <div class="tech-demo-wrapper case-demo-wrapper">
          <div
            class="demo-links"
            data-auto="demo"
            data-case="portfolio-logotype"
          ></div>
        </div>

        <nav
          class="project-switcher"
          aria-label="KT Portfolio sections"
          data-auto="switcher"
          data-case="portfolio"
        ></nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="row">
      <h2 class="case-section-heading">The brief</h2>
      <p>
        My personal identity needed to feel recognisable and handmade while
        fitting a portfolio built around fluid movement, animated blobs, and
        wave lines. The mark had to contain my initials, work as a compact
        navigation symbol, and remain useful in wider lockups and monochrome
        applications.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="row">
      <div class="case-study-grid">
        <article class="case-study-block case-study-block--challenge">
          <h2>Challenge</h2>
          <p>
            Organic marks can quickly lose clarity when scaled down. The central
            challenge was preserving the irregular ink-like silhouette while
            keeping both the K and T readable and the negative space balanced.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Combine two initials without creating a conventional monogram</li>
            <li>Suggest movement while retaining a stable silhouette</li>
            <li>Support icon, horizontal, vertical, and reversed applications</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class="case-study-item case-study-media--explore">
          <img
            class="thumb"
            src="{{base}}assets/images/logo-ktportfolio-600.webp"
            data-src="logo-ktportfolio-600.webp"
            data-path="assets/images"
            width="600"
            height="461"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            alt="Pencil explorations of a fluid KT monogram beside the refined black vector logo"
          />
          <figcaption class="case-caption">Hand-drawn exploration refined into
            the final KT symbol</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <article class="case-study-block case-study-block--decisions">
          <h2>Key decisions</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Started with loose pencil sketches to explore the initials as one
              connected shape before introducing vector precision
            </li>
            <li>
              Used an ink-splash silhouette to connect the mark with both my art
              background and the portfolio's movement-based visual language
            </li>
            <li>
              Simplified the outer contour and internal spacing so the K and T
              remain distinguishable at navigation and icon sizes
            </li>
            <li>
              Built icon-only, horizontal, vertical, text-supported, monochrome,
              and reversed variations for different contexts
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class="case-study-item case-study-media--booking">
          <img
            class="thumb"
            src="{{base}}assets/images/design-portfoliologosuite-600.webp"
            data-src="design-portfoliologosuite-600.webp"
            data-path="assets/images"
            width="600"
            height="424"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            alt="KT Portfolio logo suite showing primary, secondary, icon, vertical, outline, monochrome, and reversed variations"
          />
          <figcaption class="case-caption">Logo suite for compact navigation,
            wide layouts, and contrasting backgrounds</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="row case-outcome">
      <h2 class="case-section-heading">Outcome and reflection</h2>
      <p>
        The final mark gives the portfolio an ownable signature without relying
        on a generic developer symbol. It preserves the energy of the original
        sketches while the responsive lockups make it practical across web and
        print. The process reinforced the value of testing expressive forms at
        their smallest intended size before finalising the wider identity.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="back-to-work-container">
      <a
        href="{{base}}#work"
        class="label-link back-to-work-label"
        data-back
        data-back-fallback="{{base}}#work"
        aria-label="Back to Work"
      >
        <svg class="icon icon-18 arrow-icon" aria-hidden="true"><use
            href="#i-arrow-left"
          /></svg>
        <span class="label-text">Back to Work</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>